## Previously raised findings (review-round registry)

These defects were raised on earlier review rounds of this PR. For each finding below: verify fixed (cite the fixing hunk), withdraw (reason required), or mark re-introduced. New claims need new evidence — a claim already on this list is answered, not re-raised.

| id | status | severity | file | claim |
|----|--------|----------|------|-------|
| F1 | open | IMPORTANT | lib/src/cli/agent_cli_steering.dart | ⚠️ important the async settle can lose already consumed steering texts when a competing run starts during the persist retry window settleleftoversteering is now fire and forget unawaited at agent cli input dart 217 and the settle run branch awaits one persist re attempt per entry before starting the run agent cli steering dart 401 → 404 dart for final entry in entries final recordid await retrysteeringpersist entry unawaited steeringdelivered entry message recordid entry panel startrun texts join n the pre change settle was synchronous the running n message s now decision and startrun were one atomic block so nothing could interleave now the window between the announce line and startrun costs at least a microtask hop per entry — and a full session append up to the 2248 session io bound under a slow fs for every entry carrying a persisterror during that window isbusy is still false so a composer submit mail wake or scheduled message can start a run the settle s startrun texts join n then hits the busy guard isbusy runstarting isstreaming and refuses — after the fifo entries were already consumed pendingsteering removewhere the panels flipped to delivered and steering arrived after the last checkpoint — running n message s now was printed the steered texts end up in no run and no queue silently lost behind a running now line that lied — exactly the defect class this card closes suggested fix start the run first synchronously then fire the per entry retry deliveries — the crash recovery scan recoversessionsteering is order independent so jsonl record ordering does not constrain the swap if the retry before run ordering is deliberate steering record preceding the run s own records in the jsonl the busy guard refusal path still needs a guard re check isbusy after the awaits and re drain re drop loudly instead of consuming and losing |
| F2 | open | SUGGESTION | lib/src/cli/agent_cli_steering.dart | 💡 suggestion the text is lost can be false while the accept append is still parked in dropsettledsteering the in flight shape recordid null persisterror null prints save still in flight and then the unconditional the text is lost claim dart final why entry persisterror null save still in flight line 473 fs error entry persisterror io writeln tuiwarning ⚠ dropped steering was not saved to session why — the text is lost but retrysteeringpersist returns null for this shape without touching the session the persisterror null early return performs no writer serialized call so nothing orders the drop print behind the parked append when the parked append lands a moment later the persist resolution s index 0 branch writes the consumed marker — the text survived closed no resurrection — yet the user was told it is lost the card s remaining section argues this state is unobservable from a black box test under writer serialization but the drop branch itself does no serialized write for this entry shape so the claim doesn t hold a slow fs 2248 s bounded session io makes it reachable in production and the suite s own steeringfsgateenv holdsessionwrites across the settle can arrange it deterministically the ac2 ac3 tests park appends for 2s across boundaries already copy only no data impact — hence a suggestion either soften the in flight copy e g save still in flight — if it lands it will be marked consumed check the record before retyping or await the parked resolution bounded by the same session io bound before claiming loss |

### How to answer

Add a `findingReview` block to `outputs/pr_review.json` (alongside `inlineComments`):

```json
{"findingReview": {
  "dispositions": [
    {"n": 1, "status": "fixed", "hunk": "<file:lines \u2014 the fixing hunk>"},
    {"n": 2, "status": "withdrawn", "reason": "<why this is not a defect>"},
    {"n": 3, "status": "re-introduced"}
  ],
  "confirmations": [
    {"proposedFor": 1, "same": true}
  ]
}}
```

Unconfirmed candidates become new identities (the merge bias is confirm-only); a genuinely new finding on a resolved item is expected to be raised again with new evidence.

